# 2. Concise CYT design outline

I'd make CYT another consumer of Wavehound's existing observation architecture:

```text
                    Wi-Fi probe observations
                              │
                    BLE advertisement observations
                              │
                    AP/environment observations
                              │
                              ▼
                     ┌─────────────────┐
                     │   CYT epoch     │
                     │   accumulator   │
                     └────────┬────────┘
                              │
              ┌───────────────┴────────────────┐
              ▼                                ▼
      Device fingerprinting             RF feature vector
              │                                │
              │                        environment comparison
              │                                │
              │                         E01 → E02 → E03
              │                                │
              └───────────────┬────────────────┘
                              ▼
                     persistence tracker
                              │
                              ▼
                    ranked CYT candidates
                              │
                              ▼
                         CYT display
```

### Suggested core structs

I would **not** make these final yet; they're the conceptual shape.

```cpp
struct CYTRfFeatures {
    // Current environment
    uint16_t bssid_count;
    uint16_t bssid_turnover;
    uint16_t ssid_count;
    uint16_t ssid_turnover;

    // RSSI distribution
    int8_t  rssi_mean;
    int8_t  rssi_min;
    int8_t  rssi_max;
    uint16_t rssi_spread;

    // Channel distribution
    uint16_t channel_counts[...];

    // Additional feature fields TBD
};
```

Then:

```cpp
struct CYTEnvironment {
    uint16_t id;
    CYTRfFeatures features;

    uint32_t first_seen;
    uint32_t last_seen;
    uint32_t duration;

    uint16_t observation_count;
};
```

A device candidate:

```cpp
struct CYTDevice {
    uint64_t fingerprint_hash;

    uint8_t type;             // Wi-Fi / BLE / hybrid

    // Identity evidence
    uint8_t mac[6];
    bool has_mac;
    bool randomized_mac;

    char vendor[...];
    char ie221_vendor[...];

    // Probe/advertising evidence
    uint64_t ssid_bitmap;
    uint16_t ssid_count;
    uint32_t ie_fingerprint;

    // BLE evidence
    // address / UUID / manufacturer/service fingerprints
    // exact representation TBD

    // Persistence
    uint16_t score;
    uint16_t total_hits;
    uint32_t first_seen;
    uint32_t last_seen;

    // Environment history
    uint16_t environments_seen;
    uint16_t environment_ids[...];

    // Per-environment evidence
    uint32_t environment_duration[...];
    uint16_t environment_hits[...];
};
```

And the overall state:

```cpp
struct CYTState {
    bool active;

    uint32_t epoch_start;
    uint16_t current_environment_id;

    CYTRfFeatures current_features;

    CYTEnvironment environments[MAX_CYT_ENVIRONMENTS];
    CYTDevice devices[MAX_CYT_DEVICES];

    uint16_t environment_count;
    uint16_t device_count;
};
```

### Important architectural point

I would probably **not put arbitrarily large environment/device histories into the global state union**.

Instead, we establish explicit bounded maxima and calculate the SRAM cost before implementation.

For example:

```text
MAX_CYT_ENVIRONMENTS
MAX_CYT_DEVICES
MAX_CYT_ENV_DEVICE_HISTORY
MAX_CYT_SSID_EVIDENCE
```

The actual numbers should be determined from the WROOM's existing memory budget.

